      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Angle sum",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( A+B+C=180^{\\circ} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Exterior angle",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( =\\) sum of remote interior",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Isosceles",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "base angles equal",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Congruence",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "SSS · SAS · ASA · AAS · HL",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Two angles of a triangle are \\( 40^{\\circ} \\) and \\( 60^{\\circ} \\). Find the third.</p>\\[ 180^{\\circ}-40^{\\circ}-60^{\\circ}=80^{\\circ}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 3.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>The angles of a triangle are in the ratio \\( 1:2:3 \\). Find them.</p><p>Six parts total \\( 180^{\\circ} \\), so one part is \\( 30^{\\circ} \\):</p>\\[ 30^{\\circ},\\ 60^{\\circ},\\ 90^{\\circ}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "Common Pitfall — The 'SSA' Ambiguity Trap",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Do not attempt to prove triangles congruent using <strong>SSA</strong> (Side-Side-Angle) or <strong>AAA</strong> (Angle-Angle-Angle). SSA does not guarantee unique triangles (two non-congruent triangles can share two sides and a non-included angle, known as the ambiguous case), while AAA proves <em>similarity</em>, not congruence. The only valid congruence criteria are <strong>SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS</strong>, and <strong>HL</strong> (hypotenuse-leg in right triangles).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Exam Practice · Exterior Angle Equation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Problem:</strong> In \\( \\triangle ABC \\), the exterior angle at vertex \\( C \\) measures \\( 134^{\\circ} \\). The two remote interior angles have measures \\( m\\angle A = (5x - 4)^{\\circ} \\) and \\( m\\angle B = (3x + 10)^{\\circ} \\). Find the measure of \\( \\angle A \\).</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> By the Exterior Angle Theorem, an exterior angle equals the sum of the two remote interior angles:</p>\\[ (5x - 4) + (3x + 10) = 134 \\implies 8x + 6 = 134 \\implies 8x = 128 \\implies x = 16. \\]<p>Substitute \\( x = 16 \\) into \\( m\\angle A \\):</p>\\[ m\\angle A = 5(16) - 4 = 80 - 4 = 76^{\\circ}. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
